Package: ComPoM
Type: Package
Title: Poisson-Multinomial Models to Standardise and Scale Compositions
Version: 0.2.0
Author: Philipp Neubauer, D'Arcy Webber
Maintainer: Philipp Neubauer <philipp@dragonfly.co.nz>
Description: Fits Poisson-multinomial models to compositional count data, such
    as length or age frequencies from fisheries observer or market sampling, in
    order to standardise compositions for non-representative sampling and scale
    them to total catch. Counts per bin and sampling stratum are modelled as
    Poisson with the stratum sample size as offset, and all covariate effects
    enter as bin-specific terms, so that the implied proportions within a
    stratum follow a multinomial-logit model. Effects of interest (e.g. year)
    are fitted as bin-specific fixed effects, nuisance sampling factors (e.g.
    vessel, season) as bin-specific independent random effects, and continuous
    covariates (e.g. sea-surface temperature) as smooths over covariate and
    bin. Models are fitted with 'brms' (Bayesian, via Stan) or 'sdmTMB'
    (maximum likelihood, via TMB). The 'sdmTMB' backend can add bin-specific
    spatial and spatio-temporal Gaussian random fields through the SPDE
    approach. Fitted models are used to predict compositions for strata with
    known catch, which are then aggregated to catch-weighted compositions with
    uncertainty from posterior or simulation draws. Plotting functions show
    estimated effects, posterior predictive checks, smooth effects, spatial
    fields, and scaled compositions.
